1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:06,520
Throughout the bloody battle for the Pacific, one class of fighting ship was always in the thick of the action.

2
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:11,540
Its creed was to go in harm's way, and its motto, kill or be killed.

3
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:18,680
From protecting the capital ships and bombarding enemy troops, to sinking submarines and bringing down the dreaded kamikaze,

4
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,380
it took on the full ferocity of the Japanese and won.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:28,420
Always fighting at close range, these ships were the eyes, ears, and teeth of the American Navy.

6
00:00:28,421 --> 00:00:31,720
Now, The Destroyer on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:42,000
April 9th, 1942.

8
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:45,880
Japanese troops capture the Philippine peninsula of Bataan.

9
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:51,420
More than 70,000 Filipino and American troops are taken captive.

10
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:54,940
It is the largest U.S. army in history to surrender.

11
00:00:54,941 --> 00:00:58,140
And one of the blackest days of the war.

12
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:04,140
General MacArthur vowed that one day he would return and retake the islands.

13
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,460
But that day seemed a long way away.

14
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:11,060
Japan appeared invincible.

15
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:18,200
Poised to sweep across the entire Pacific, a shudder of fear reverberated throughout the free world.

16
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:26,621
But on that same dark day, a ship with a radical new design was undergoing trials for the U.S.

17
00:01:26,622 --> 00:01:27,322
Navy.

18
00:01:27,323 --> 00:01:31,640
It would be the first of the most successful fighting ships of World War II,

19
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,800
the new Fletcher-class destroyers.

20
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:42,080
The genesis for the destroyer was in the 1870s,

21
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:45,320
when motor torpedo boats were used to attack battleships.

22
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,740
Relatively small but extremely fast,

23
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,760
they were usually armed with a single torpedo tube

24
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:55,300
and some form of rapid-fire machine gun.

25
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:02,960
By the outbreak of World War I, the torpedo boat had evolved into the destroyer.

26
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,240
It had become the backbone of navies throughout the world.

27
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:11,720
But in the 1920s, Japan launched a new class of destroyers.

28
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,180
The West took notice.

29
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:21,560
These fast, heavily armed ships with five-inch guns were stronger than any ship in a Western fleet.

30
00:02:21,561 --> 00:02:31,180
as Japan's expansionist policies grew so did her fleet soon powerful battleships carriers and

31
00:02:31,181 --> 00:02:36,800
cruisers were churning out of dockyards Germany and Italy were also determined to

32
00:02:36,801 --> 00:02:40,460
build up their fleets ship after ship took to the seas

33
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:50,420
but in America little was done to counter these threats the design of the American destroyer

34
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:56,060
really was static into the 20s and early 30s.

35
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,600
No ships were built.

36
00:02:58,300 --> 00:02:59,460
Many were decommissioned.

37
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:03,540
So basically, the type of destroyer that the U.S. Navy had

38
00:03:03,541 --> 00:03:06,280
into the 20s and 30s was the World War I emergency program,

39
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,060
Flesh Deckers.

40
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:11,680
As the world entered a frenzied arms race,

41
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,680
American naval designers finally looked closely

42
00:03:14,681 --> 00:03:15,700
at these new fleets.

43
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:18,880
What they saw was alarming.

44
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:24,640
America had to come up with a fast and heavily armed destroyer of her own.

45
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:30,580
The designers also faced an additional problem, enemy aircraft.

46
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:34,500
Planes were becoming faster and more powerful.

47
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:41,300
New destroyers would need enough firepower to not only protect themselves, but also destroy the aircraft.

48
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:50,120
In 1939, plans were laid down to build a new class of American destroyer, the Fletcher

49
00:03:50,121 --> 00:03:53,340
class.

50
00:03:53,341 --> 00:03:58,160
These would be the largest destroyers ever built by any nation.

51
00:03:58,161 --> 00:04:03,340
The design was an engineering achievement, a balance of ruggedness and seaworthiness,

52
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:08,640
armament and speed on a classically proportioned steel hull.

53
00:04:08,641 --> 00:04:13,840
376 feet long and nearly 2,900 tons when fully loaded.

54
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:18,740
The Fletchers were significantly larger than any previous American destroyer.

55
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,840
They had five 5-inch guns,

56
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:26,320
10 21-inch torpedo tubes,

57
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:29,760
7 40-millimeter guns,

58
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:32,160
and 7 20-millimeter guns.

59
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:37,300
And they were armed with 56 depth charges.

60
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:48,240
They were also the first destroyer fitted with surface and air warning radar.

61
00:04:48,241 --> 00:04:54,200
With their 60,000 shaft horsepower engines delivering a speed of over 36 knots, the destroyer

62
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:57,940
was destined to be an awesome fighting machine.

63
00:04:57,941 --> 00:05:02,600
World events would soon prove it.

64
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:07,100
In September 1939, Germany invaded Poland.

65
00:05:07,101 --> 00:05:17,460
In 1940, German U-boats began decimating British convoys in the Atlantic.

66
00:05:17,461 --> 00:05:23,900
America was still neutral, but one incident would bring her closer to war.

67
00:05:23,901 --> 00:05:30,900
On September 4, 1941, during a routine convoy patrol, an American World War I destroyer,

68
00:05:30,901 --> 00:05:34,220
the Greer had two torpedoes fired at her by a U-boat.

69
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:41,100
The U-boat fired torpedoes at the Greer,

70
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:44,320
which in turn responded with depth charges.

71
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:48,580
Greer, of course, had been shadowing the submarine,

72
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:51,320
and so the German U-boat skipper,

73
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:54,600
in defending his ship, fired torpedoes.

74
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:59,460
Although the Greer was not hit,

75
00:05:59,461 --> 00:06:02,600
President Roosevelt sent a chilling message to Germany.

76
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:06,580
With millions of Americans listening,

77
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:10,040
he issued a shoot-on-site order to the U.S. Navy

78
00:06:10,041 --> 00:06:12,940
on any German vessel found in American waters.

79
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:17,200
But let this warning be clear.

80
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:22,520
From now on, if German or Italian vessels of war

81
00:06:22,521 --> 00:06:24,620
enter the waters,

82
00:06:24,621 --> 00:06:29,300
the protection of which is necessary for American defense,

83
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:33,000
They do so at their own peril.

84
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,587
Shortly afterwards, another U.S.

85
00:06:36,588 --> 00:06:41,180
destroyer, the USS Kearney, was badly damaged by a torpedo attack.

86
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:46,060
Eleven American sailors were lost, the first U.S. deaths by Germans.

87
00:06:47,900 --> 00:06:50,960
America had been bloodied in a war that she was not fighting.

88
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:55,980
The U.S. Navy's destroyers had fired America's first shots of the war.

89
00:06:55,981 --> 00:06:59,360
This was just the beginning.

90
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:06,580
A full-scale world war would soon erupt with a destroyer at the tip of the spear.

91
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:21,600
On September 4th, 1941, while patrolling south of Iceland, the USS Greer was attacked by a German U-boat.

92
00:07:21,601 --> 00:07:28,680
The Greer evaded two torpedoes fired at her and became the first American ship in World War II to attack the Germans.

93
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:32,420
The Destroyer will return on Modern Marvels.

94
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:39,880
Sunday, December 7th, 1941.

95
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:45,000
Japan hurled its aircraft at the American Pacific Fleet in Pearl Harbor.

96
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,660
Within 55 minutes, the fleet lay in ruins.

97
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:53,560
America was at war.

98
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:04,600
Immediately, the might of the U.S. industry mobilized behind the war effort.

99
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:09,500
The Pacific fleet had been severely damaged.

100
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:12,860
The country needed ships to take the fight back to Japan.

101
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:19,900
Across America, shipyards sprang into action to build the hardware that would avenge Pearl Harbor.

102
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:28,240
Eleven shipyards were given the task to build the new destroyers.

103
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:40,440
As ship after ship rolled off the production line, the call went out for sailors to man them.

104
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:46,780
I was 17 years old, and I enlisted eight days after turning 17.

105
00:08:47,540 --> 00:08:51,200
And about three days later, I was sworn in and on my way to San Diego.

106
00:08:51,600 --> 00:09:01,907
I had been to boy scout camp, but it was a far cry from that because it was, everything went by the book, it went by the bell, it went by the whistle, and the chief

107
00:09:01,908 --> 00:09:10,266
made us march up and down, and we had to go to bed at a certain time and get up at a certain time, take a shower every day and shave.

108
00:09:10,267 --> 00:09:14,100
We're 17 years old, shaving. I didn't have anything to shave.

109
00:09:14,101 --> 00:09:18,940
Straight from boot camp, these men, some as young as 16,

110
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:22,060
were sent to the U.S. Navy's most advanced destroyers.

111
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:25,120
For many, it would be love at first sight.

112
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,160
Well, I loved it immediately.

113
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:30,520
That's called the Dungaree Navy.

114
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:36,540
All one crew, really, officers and men were one group together.

115
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:40,600
Destroyer is a special thing unto itself.

116
00:09:40,601 --> 00:09:46,440
It has the speed and it throbs and you know you're at sea on those things.

117
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:49,940
So it was something I found exhilarating.

118
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:54,800
But the bloody fight for the Pacific had intensified.

119
00:09:56,780 --> 00:09:59,180
Japan conquered everything before her.

120
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,520
American forces had to control the seas before they could retake the islands.

121
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:07,820
The destroyer would be in the thick of the battles.

122
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:13,840
June 1942, midway.

123
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,742
In an unforgiving battle, the U.S.

124
00:10:17,743 --> 00:10:22,180
Navy's aircraft sank four of Japan's frontline aircraft carriers.

125
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:24,480
But at a price.

126
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:31,320
The carrier USS Yorktown was also sunk.

127
00:10:31,321 --> 00:10:40,760
at the very same hour seven time zones away the first of the new breed of destroyers was ready

128
00:10:40,761 --> 00:10:46,240
to enter the fight for the pacific but its crewmen would soon find out the conditions

129
00:10:46,241 --> 00:10:52,060
aboard a u.s destroyer were a far cry from the comfort enjoyed aboard the bigger capital ships

130
00:10:52,061 --> 00:11:00,360
complement of the ship was slightly over 300 officers and and crew and the officers they

131
00:11:00,361 --> 00:11:07,760
lived to a stateroom and the enlisted people who lived 50, 60 in a compartment and the

132
00:11:07,761 --> 00:11:10,586
bunks were just big enough for one person.

133
00:11:10,587 --> 00:11:13,480
You didn't turn over too much because you'd

134
00:11:13,481 --> 00:11:19,082
fall out. They were stacked either three high or four high.

135
00:11:19,083 --> 00:11:21,740
I was on the bottom bunk, so

136
00:11:21,741 --> 00:11:27,940
I was maybe this high off the deck, which meant I could get up easy, just roll out and

137
00:11:27,941 --> 00:11:28,660
and start going.

138
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:36,140
When everything you own goes in a little foot locker that big,

139
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:40,240
you know, it doesn't seem like you're needing space.

140
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:41,480
You know, you've got plenty of room.

141
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:46,000
And as long as you've got your bunk and mattress,

142
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:47,180
well, you're in pretty good shape.

143
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:49,060
Heat was tremendous.

144
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:52,800
Of course, you were constantly soaking wet in the tropics.

145
00:11:52,801 --> 00:11:56,460
And on deck, it was always well into hundreds.

146
00:11:56,461 --> 00:11:59,020
and you had to try to keep in the shade.

147
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:03,900
If you were away from the landmass and moving at a fairly good clip,

148
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:06,680
it wasn't too bad because you had the air hitting you.

149
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:10,360
But once you got into the channel there around Guadalcanal,

150
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:11,900
the heat was just terrible there.

151
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:15,020
Hotter than blue blazes.

152
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:19,640
And most of us went up on deck to sleep.

153
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:22,640
And I can remember clearly being on the deck.

154
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:25,700
And when the five-inch gun would go off,

155
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:32,320
I'd come up off the deck and bounce down and then go right back to sleep.

156
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:37,500
The food, you know, of course we had good food.

157
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:40,080
You got a little bit tired of it.

158
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:44,400
The old saying used to be that they had the best food in the world until the cooks got it.

159
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:48,180
And then they changed it all, but they did a good job.

160
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:52,720
And we ate real good most of the time.

161
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:54,500
The food was horrible.

162
00:12:54,501 --> 00:12:55,780
you know

163
00:12:55,781 --> 00:12:58,420
I think we had a bad cook

164
00:12:58,421 --> 00:13:00,160
you know, and nobody ever

165
00:13:00,161 --> 00:13:02,500
gave three cheers for the cook on our ship

166
00:13:02,501 --> 00:13:04,500
I'll tell you, they called out

167
00:13:04,501 --> 00:13:06,280
which watch was

168
00:13:06,281 --> 00:13:08,400
ready for lunch

169
00:13:08,401 --> 00:13:09,380
or dinner or whatever

170
00:13:09,381 --> 00:13:11,460
and of course they just called a chow

171
00:13:11,461 --> 00:13:14,200
and you got in line, went through with a

172
00:13:14,201 --> 00:13:16,460
metal tray and they threw the food on the

173
00:13:16,461 --> 00:13:18,140
tray and then you sat down

174
00:13:18,141 --> 00:13:19,180
and ate, you know

175
00:13:19,181 --> 00:13:22,180
I can't remember having but one or two

176
00:13:22,181 --> 00:13:24,160
good meals all the time I was

177
00:13:24,161 --> 00:13:24,960
on that ship.

178
00:13:28,860 --> 00:13:32,040
For the officers, life on board a destroyer

179
00:13:32,041 --> 00:13:33,200
was much more comfortable.

180
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:37,400
The officers lived pretty darn well.

181
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,640
By tradition, they had Filipino mess boys

182
00:13:40,641 --> 00:13:44,220
in white jackets, a ward room,

183
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:48,560
a ward room table and a couple of places to sit down.

184
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:53,420
We were served on china and silver.

185
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:58,480
But the conditions in the washroom were another story.

186
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:13,920
Aft, where I lived, we had two showers and, oh, maybe two dozen lavatories and six toilets.

187
00:14:13,921 --> 00:14:20,240
and the toilets were a trough with planks across the trough to sit on

188
00:14:20,241 --> 00:14:24,220
and seawater running in one end and out the other.

189
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:28,300
And, of course, the ship rolled all the time and pitched,

190
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:31,960
and it was always water on the deck, so your feet always got wet.

191
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:37,120
When you went into a shower booth, there'd be four other guys already in there.

192
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:41,260
So what you did there was kind of lather up

193
00:14:41,261 --> 00:14:49,980
and get splashed from these other fellows and then hopefully get enough water on you to get

194
00:14:49,981 --> 00:14:55,740
rid of soap so there's always maybe eight or ten naked men standing around either getting wet or

195
00:14:55,741 --> 00:15:02,540
soaping or getting rinsed or whatever it was uh it was an experience

196
00:15:02,541 --> 00:15:12,920
but in the washroom there was one place no one wanted to be on the red seat back then

197
00:15:12,921 --> 00:15:21,800
during World War II and before sailors who had a venereal disease they were considered to have

198
00:15:21,801 --> 00:15:27,140
stolen time from the Navy because they couldn't have certain duty and they were set aside and

199
00:15:27,141 --> 00:15:32,080
And whatever time that they lost, they had to tack it on at the end of the enlistment

200
00:15:32,081 --> 00:15:35,320
so that the government didn't lose anything.

201
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:39,560
And so rather than have them use the same facilities that everybody else had

202
00:15:39,561 --> 00:15:43,260
because they might catch something, which of course you couldn't, but we didn't know that,

203
00:15:43,740 --> 00:15:46,680
they had a separate toilet for that sort of thing.

204
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:53,760
In the fall of 1942, Fletcher-class destroyers arrived in Guadalcanal

205
00:15:53,761 --> 00:15:57,520
and formed an elite fighting unit known as the Cactus Strike Force.

206
00:15:58,360 --> 00:15:59,880
It wasn't a moment too soon.

207
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,680
Japanese forces were continuing to advance.

208
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:09,020
The technology, engineering, and armament of the destroyer had to pay off.

209
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:11,240
Lives depended on it.

210
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:18,460
The first U.S. destroyer, the USS Bainbridge, was commissioned on November 24, 1902.

211
00:16:18,461 --> 00:16:24,380
The Bainbridge was actually a torpedo boat destroyer that was 250 feet in length,

212
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:28,940
displaced 420 tons, and had a speed of 29 knots.

213
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:30,960
She carried a crew of 75.

214
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,680
The destroyer will return on Modern Marvels.

215
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:43,380
By early 1943, the first five Fletcher-class destroyers had arrived in the Pacific.

216
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:45,960
They were known as the Cactus Strike Force.

217
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:51,440
Their first job was to target and smash the Japanese supply convoys,

218
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:54,840
ferrying troops used in the invasions of the Pacific Islands.

219
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:58,420
It's something that's hard to describe.

220
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:02,560
You have no lights. Not any ship has a light showing.

221
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:09,640
And there were all sorts of navigational hazards which you thought of.

222
00:17:09,641 --> 00:17:15,760
As a matter of fact, we were using some charts that dated back to the 1700s.

223
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:20,600
We got very close, within 60 miles of the Japanese airfield,

224
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:22,060
so that's why we had to do it at night.

225
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:27,940
And they were coming down mainly to resupply their troops,

226
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:34,200
and they had a sophisticated large system that would hug the shoreline and go at night.

227
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:41,520
So our job was to intercept them and try to cut them off,

228
00:17:42,060 --> 00:17:44,760
and their job was to get bias.

229
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:48,880
Already, the role of the destroyers was changing.

230
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:53,600
At first, they were considered only as defense for the capital ships.

231
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:57,280
Now, the Fletchers were being thrown into the furnace of battle.

232
00:17:59,780 --> 00:18:04,520
If there was a strong point you wanted reduced, you called for fire from the ships offshore,

233
00:18:04,700 --> 00:18:07,120
and many times it was a destroyer that provided that.

234
00:18:10,360 --> 00:18:14,100
They would screen the larger ships, the battleships, carriers, cruisers,

235
00:18:14,101 --> 00:18:17,160
performing such duties as lifeguard.

236
00:18:17,700 --> 00:18:20,760
Say if a pilot went down or a plane crashed a stern of the ship,

237
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:23,120
the destroyer would be there to pick the pilot up,

238
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:28,000
usually rewarded by the carrier with a gallon of ice cream or two for their efforts.

239
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,380
They performed convoy escort.

240
00:18:31,180 --> 00:18:34,840
In short, any duty that needed a fast ship to go in harm's way

241
00:18:34,841 --> 00:18:36,780
that usually were the Fletchers ended up.

242
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:39,760
The destroyer was a floating gun platform.

243
00:18:39,761 --> 00:18:44,500
The five 5-inch guns and their devastating firepower were its main armament.

244
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:52,780
The 5-inch .38 could fire a 54-pound projectile 18,000 yards,

245
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:57,360
which is about 9 nautical miles, about 10 1⁄2 statue miles.

246
00:18:58,320 --> 00:19:04,840
It had a ceiling of over 35,000 feet for any aircraft.

247
00:19:04,841 --> 00:19:12,800
then of course it could be used rapid fire for beach invasions and that sort of thing

248
00:19:12,801 --> 00:19:19,520
ammunition from the magazines below deck was fed to the guns as fast as they could fire them

249
00:19:19,521 --> 00:19:25,960
we fired three every three seconds but they had to qualify that because that if everything goes

250
00:19:25,961 --> 00:19:33,240
proper these men laying these big projectiles in the tray they can't hold up too long loading of

251
00:19:33,241 --> 00:19:39,240
that fast.

252
00:19:39,241 --> 00:19:44,560
Those three to four seconds was pretty good for five or ten rounds.

253
00:19:44,561 --> 00:19:49,620
After that, your fire slows down.

254
00:19:49,621 --> 00:19:50,620
It's dirty.

255
00:19:50,621 --> 00:19:56,860
You'd be surprised that every time the gun fires, you have grease flying around and gun

256
00:19:56,861 --> 00:19:57,860
powder.

257
00:19:57,861 --> 00:20:02,420
You come out, you're just peppered with grease all over your face and clothes.

258
00:20:02,820 --> 00:20:04,920
And very smoky, very noisy.

259
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:14,960
The Fletcher's 40 and 20-millimeter anti-aircraft guns put up a shield of steel against enemy bombers and fighters.

260
00:20:15,620 --> 00:20:19,540
The 40-millimeter rate of fire was 160 rounds per minute.

261
00:20:19,541 --> 00:20:26,220
The 20-millimeter, a staggering 450 rounds per minute, enough to shred an aircraft in seconds.

262
00:20:27,860 --> 00:20:35,060
I was a loader on a 40-millimeter gun, which was a good position to be in.

263
00:20:35,061 --> 00:20:41,000
The problem was you were so busy loading that you couldn't really look at the plane that was coming in.

264
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:45,780
And if you did, there was always a chance of you losing your concentration by getting that gun loaded.

265
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:48,800
So I tried not to look at the planes coming in.

266
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:56,040
A command center that controlled all this firepower was deep in the ship.

267
00:20:57,040 --> 00:21:02,220
Packed with the latest electronic radar, it could locate the enemy up to 40 miles away.

268
00:21:03,380 --> 00:21:05,160
It was known as the CIC.

269
00:21:07,420 --> 00:21:10,300
CIC is the Combat Information Center.

270
00:21:11,260 --> 00:21:16,540
And all communications and all the radar returns, all the radar equipment,

271
00:21:16,980 --> 00:21:22,720
and most of the radio equipment, too, was centered in the Combat Information Center.

272
00:21:22,721 --> 00:21:26,820
and all during any action of any sort,

273
00:21:27,380 --> 00:21:29,620
that's where everything was kept track of.

274
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:34,960
With its radar tracking anything that moved above the surface,

275
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:38,400
the Fletchers also had the most sophisticated sonar,

276
00:21:38,860 --> 00:21:41,860
enabling them to hunt down anything that was below the sea.

277
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:47,020
As a sonarman, my assignment was to go up on the bridge

278
00:21:47,021 --> 00:21:48,640
where the sonar equipment was,

279
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:51,160
and we sent out a signal underwater,

280
00:21:51,161 --> 00:21:53,180
a sound signal

281
00:21:53,181 --> 00:21:56,540
and it went out until it hit something

282
00:21:56,541 --> 00:21:59,460
and we could tell by the time

283
00:21:59,461 --> 00:22:00,720
that the signal went out

284
00:22:00,721 --> 00:22:01,960
and the echo came back

285
00:22:01,961 --> 00:22:03,480
we would get the distance

286
00:22:03,481 --> 00:22:06,060
and so we knew where it was

287
00:22:06,061 --> 00:22:07,780
then the officer says

288
00:22:07,781 --> 00:22:09,160
to the telephone talker

289
00:22:09,161 --> 00:22:09,861
fire one

290
00:22:09,862 --> 00:22:14,580
boom it goes out

291
00:22:14,581 --> 00:22:15,360
then fire two

292
00:22:15,361 --> 00:22:16,061
fire three

293
00:22:16,062 --> 00:22:18,020
and so they're going out

294
00:22:18,021 --> 00:22:19,260
on both sides of the ship

295
00:22:19,261 --> 00:22:26,780
three side racks of depth chargers and you have two stern racks and two of them are going off at

296
00:22:26,781 --> 00:22:32,700
a time the side racks are 300 pounds of tnt and the stern is 600 pounds each so you're putting a

297
00:22:32,701 --> 00:22:38,280
lot of a lot of explosives down there and it doesn't have to land on deck it just has to get

298
00:22:38,281 --> 00:22:43,860
close because it'll open seams and once you open the seam in the submarine there's not much else

299
00:22:43,861 --> 00:22:51,300
you can do sink and sink they did the Fletcher class destroyer sank 29

300
00:22:51,301 --> 00:22:56,700
Japanese submarines but sinking a sub was not enough you had to have proof of a

301
00:22:56,701 --> 00:23:03,360
kill to determine the kill well we put a whale boat over the side and send crew

302
00:23:03,361 --> 00:23:06,580
around see what they can pick up and pick up a lot of flotsam sometimes it

303
00:23:06,581 --> 00:23:09,140
pick up body parts.

304
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:12,640
The second submarine we sank,

305
00:23:13,080 --> 00:23:14,920
we found a piece of lung,

306
00:23:15,040 --> 00:23:17,060
and the doctor aboard examined it

307
00:23:17,061 --> 00:23:18,660
and said the man had tuberculosis.

308
00:23:19,620 --> 00:23:21,300
Almost in advanced stage.

309
00:23:23,540 --> 00:23:26,520
The destroyers were always in the thick of action,

310
00:23:26,860 --> 00:23:28,160
fighting at close range.

311
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:30,460
Their losses began to mount.

312
00:23:31,680 --> 00:23:34,160
On the night of February 1st, 1943,

313
00:23:34,161 --> 00:23:39,400
the USS Dehaven was spotted by enemy aircraft off the coast of Guadalcanal.

314
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:44,400
The Japanese thought Dehaven was an interception force sent to block their evacuation.

315
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:48,480
They hurled a full squadron of dive bombers at the destroyer.

316
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:55,220
I remember hearing or knowing that there was conversation over the TBS to talk between ships,

317
00:23:55,221 --> 00:24:01,480
that there were bogeys in the area and they were suspected of being Japanese planes.

318
00:24:01,481 --> 00:24:09,660
but our captain for some reason decided to to wait to wait to wait to see if if further

319
00:24:09,661 --> 00:24:17,560
identification of these planes could be made and he waited i guess too long and i stepped out on

320
00:24:17,561 --> 00:24:22,800
the starboard wing of the bridge and looked back aft across the top of the stacks and i saw the

321
00:24:22,801 --> 00:24:30,940
first bomber come in and i watched the bomb being released from beneath the plane it was that it was

322
00:24:30,941 --> 00:24:35,320
that close and I could see it it's still a burnt orange color in my mind look

323
00:24:35,321 --> 00:24:40,900
like the size of a of a large ball and I at that instant I said I shouldn't be

324
00:24:40,901 --> 00:24:47,200
out here in the open so I jumped inside very quickly into the sonar room and a

325
00:24:47,201 --> 00:24:50,780
moment later the explosion went off

326
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:59,040
I think the second bomb that hit knocked out the engine room

327
00:24:59,041 --> 00:25:04,720
And, of course, the third one, what had to be the one that hit the number two gun mount,

328
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:07,620
went down and set off the powder magazine down at the bottom of the ship

329
00:25:07,621 --> 00:25:13,780
to blow the bow off and just blasted the pilot house and the superstructure, pushed it back.

330
00:25:15,200 --> 00:25:17,020
I managed to pull myself loose.

331
00:25:17,380 --> 00:25:21,620
I just lowered myself about three feet into the water, swam away.

332
00:25:22,880 --> 00:25:27,160
I saw no other person until I was farther away from the ship

333
00:25:27,161 --> 00:25:29,940
and I realized there were other heads bobbing around in the water.

334
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:34,480
And I looked back, perhaps a few minutes later,

335
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:37,240
and watched the ship slide under the water.

336
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:43,680
There were 167 men killed and 38 or 39 wounded.

337
00:25:46,940 --> 00:25:49,720
The Dehaven was the first destroyer lost

338
00:25:49,721 --> 00:25:54,100
and the 15th American ship sunk in the bloody Guadalcanal campaign.

339
00:25:54,101 --> 00:25:58,420
unknown to the men of the destroyers

340
00:25:58,421 --> 00:26:00,100
it was just the beginning

341
00:26:00,101 --> 00:26:02,320
darker days were coming

342
00:26:02,321 --> 00:26:04,040
for the ships of the pacific

343
00:26:04,041 --> 00:26:05,740
and their crews

344
00:26:05,741 --> 00:26:08,840
the guadalcanal campaign

345
00:26:08,841 --> 00:26:10,880
marked the first significant strategic

346
00:26:10,881 --> 00:26:12,400
combined arms victory

347
00:26:12,401 --> 00:26:14,800
by the allies over japan and the pacific

348
00:26:14,801 --> 00:26:17,020
for this reason it is often referred to

349
00:26:17,021 --> 00:26:18,600
as a turning point in the war

350
00:26:18,601 --> 00:26:21,000
modern marvels will return in a moment

351
00:26:21,001 --> 00:26:32,760
By the spring of 1943, the Fletcher-class destroyer had established itself as a relentless, multi-role attack machine against the Japanese.

352
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:38,820
On constant ready alert, each man's nerves were on edge, waiting the dreaded call.

353
00:26:39,260 --> 00:26:42,080
General quarters, general quarters, make your battle station.

354
00:26:42,081 --> 00:26:43,586
It was a little bit scary.

355
00:26:43,587 --> 00:26:48,740
You know, you'd grab, usually you left your helmet up at the gun, at your battle station.

356
00:26:48,741 --> 00:26:50,620
and, you know, it was hooked onto the gun up there.

357
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:53,540
So you'd just grab, if you were dressed, you'd...

358
00:26:53,541 --> 00:26:55,200
went like you were.

359
00:26:55,300 --> 00:26:58,520
If you weren't dressed, you'd try to throw a pair of pants on

360
00:26:58,521 --> 00:27:00,000
and get up there as quick as you could.

361
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:02,600
And you'd go up and man your station,

362
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:05,240
and then just wait from there to see what was going to happen next.

363
00:27:08,480 --> 00:27:10,260
With the call to General Quarters,

364
00:27:10,261 --> 00:27:12,960
the men of the destroyers knew they might be sunk.

365
00:27:13,440 --> 00:27:15,900
They had a special way of protecting their valuables.

366
00:27:15,901 --> 00:27:21,360
The few little valuables we had, you know, we were always thinking what to do.

367
00:27:22,440 --> 00:27:27,940
We would put them in a rubber, we called them in those days, and today they call them condoms.

368
00:27:28,860 --> 00:27:35,500
But anyway, we would put all our little valuables in there and then tie a knot in this thing and keep it in our pocket.

369
00:27:38,500 --> 00:27:42,160
On every front, they were involved in close contact battle.

370
00:27:42,161 --> 00:27:45,840
Every weapon was used to destroy the enemy.

371
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:53,840
But perhaps one of the strangest weapons was used on the night of April 5th, 1943.

372
00:27:55,720 --> 00:28:00,020
The USS O'Bannon and the other destroyers of the Cactus Strike Force

373
00:28:00,021 --> 00:28:03,720
were returning from a mission bombarding Japanese shore positions

374
00:28:03,721 --> 00:28:07,660
when the O'Bannon picked up a large Japanese submarine on her sonar.

375
00:28:07,661 --> 00:28:12,640
everybody looked and sure enough there was a submarine sitting on the surface

376
00:28:12,641 --> 00:28:20,100
we had a commodore aboard and he wanted to ram that submarine and the captain didn't want to

377
00:28:20,101 --> 00:28:25,780
fearing that a collision could cause an explosion the o'bannon's captain ordered a hard change of

378
00:28:25,781 --> 00:28:31,380
course as the two ships came within feet of each other the japanese were suddenly alerted to the

379
00:28:31,381 --> 00:28:32,081
situation.

380
00:28:32,082 --> 00:28:37,340
We got right up to them and they paid no attention to us and we were running right

381
00:28:37,341 --> 00:28:42,717
alongside of them when they woke up and jumped up and started running around it.

382
00:28:42,718 --> 00:28:43,860
Nobody could fire

383
00:28:43,861 --> 00:28:49,820
anything. Our smallest guns could not come down to fire on the submarine either.

384
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:56,440
So some of the fellows picked potatoes out of the bins which were right in and started

385
00:28:56,441 --> 00:28:58,760
throwing them because there they were, you know.

386
00:29:01,380 --> 00:29:06,380
and the Japanese evidently thought they were hand grenades,

387
00:29:06,580 --> 00:29:09,360
and so they ran and started throwing them back

388
00:29:09,361 --> 00:29:12,980
and throwing them off the submarine before they would blow up.

389
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:16,920
And in that period of time, they did not get to their gun,

390
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:20,100
and we were able to pull away from them and start firing.

391
00:29:21,320 --> 00:29:23,560
And then we came back and made a run on them,

392
00:29:23,561 --> 00:29:27,880
and evidently we learned later that we actually did sink the submarine.

393
00:29:27,881 --> 00:29:33,240
A fitting tribute to the men of the O'Bannon was delivered in the form of a plaque.

394
00:29:33,860 --> 00:29:36,100
It was sent by the potato growers of Maine.

395
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:43,220
The relentless fight for the Pacific continued through 1943.

396
00:29:44,260 --> 00:29:49,920
By 1944, the destroyers had been involved in some of the bloodiest battles of World War II.

397
00:29:50,300 --> 00:29:56,380
But it was during the Battle of Leyte Gulf that the fighting men of the Tin Cans became legends.

398
00:29:57,880 --> 00:30:08,020
On October 25th, four Fletcher-class destroyers were on escort duty, protecting a task force involved in landing operations at Leyte Gulf.

399
00:30:08,940 --> 00:30:13,260
Two of these destroyers were the USS Johnston and the USS Hole.

400
00:30:14,420 --> 00:30:22,560
When the Johnston had been commissioned, her captain, Commander Ernest Evans, made a speech to his crew that typified the destroyer man's creed.

401
00:30:23,340 --> 00:30:25,520
This is going to be a fighting ship, he said.

402
00:30:25,521 --> 00:30:32,000
I intend to go in harm's way, and anyone who doesn't want to go along had better get off right now.

403
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:36,560
I said he had more courage than was good for the rest of us.

404
00:30:38,740 --> 00:30:43,340
The men of the Johnston and Hole thought they were protected from any surface attack

405
00:30:43,341 --> 00:30:45,780
by the ships of Admiral Halsey's 7th Fleet

406
00:30:45,781 --> 00:30:49,760
and were completely surprised when heavy shells started to land around them.

407
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:55,600
The two destroyers were in the gun sites of the Japanese Navy's center fleet.

408
00:30:57,340 --> 00:31:01,540
There were four battleships in that Japanese fleet,

409
00:31:01,800 --> 00:31:05,140
eight cruisers and 11 or more destroyers.

410
00:31:06,100 --> 00:31:11,400
And the Yamato was the largest battleship in the world.

411
00:31:11,820 --> 00:31:15,360
They had 18.1-inch guns, and they had nine of them.

412
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:19,720
And one projectile weighed about 3,400 pounds.

413
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:23,980
compared to our 54 pounds for a 5-inch projectile.

414
00:31:25,600 --> 00:31:29,740
Captain Evans, who had promised his men that they were going into harm's way,

415
00:31:30,160 --> 00:31:33,020
led his ship in a David and Goliath heroic attack.

416
00:31:33,920 --> 00:31:37,360
The first order I heard him give was all ahead flank,

417
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:40,660
which meant as fast as you could go.

418
00:31:41,080 --> 00:31:44,220
We began making smoke, zigzagging,

419
00:31:44,221 --> 00:31:50,600
So he told our gunnery officer to pick out one of those lead cruisers.

420
00:31:51,560 --> 00:31:52,480
And we did.

421
00:31:52,960 --> 00:31:55,180
And we made the torpedo run on it.

422
00:31:55,380 --> 00:32:01,380
And I know I stood over on the starboard side and watched all ten torpedoes as they were fired.

423
00:32:01,381 --> 00:32:15,381
We completed our run and then we turned and we was trying to retire into our own smoke

424
00:32:18,760 --> 00:32:24,820
and then we was hit for the first time with the three 14-inch projectiles, mostly a midship.

425
00:32:24,821 --> 00:32:33,500
the ship just completely rose up out of the water and came down with a thud

426
00:32:33,501 --> 00:32:42,280
that projectile it's got a whoo whoo like that and it's a terrible noise

427
00:32:42,281 --> 00:32:50,080
for some reason i just turned and and started walking forward and i only got just a few feet

428
00:32:50,081 --> 00:32:54,080
And his round came in and killed most of those guys that I was standing with.

429
00:32:54,720 --> 00:32:58,520
And I look back and I recall, I can still see it today,

430
00:32:58,860 --> 00:33:03,340
one of my friends from about half up, and his arms are still moving.

431
00:33:04,560 --> 00:33:08,240
Helpless in the water and pounded by the Japanese heavy guns,

432
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:11,220
the Johnston turned to help its fellow destroyer.

433
00:33:11,960 --> 00:33:14,700
Its young crew members began to think the unthinkable.

434
00:33:15,260 --> 00:33:19,380
And I truly believed that that was the last day of my life.

435
00:33:20,080 --> 00:33:25,040
With the Johnston dead in the water, Captain Evans had no option but to abandon ship.

436
00:33:26,040 --> 00:33:29,320
It was a bad feeling.

437
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:33,800
First of all, you know, you'd never seen anything like you saw that day,

438
00:33:34,220 --> 00:33:37,840
and you was one of those that thought, this can't happen to me, you know.

439
00:33:39,500 --> 00:33:42,860
Yeah, it wasn't a good feeling at all.

440
00:33:44,200 --> 00:33:49,180
As the survivors huddled together on life rafts, they thought that rescue would come swiftly.

441
00:33:50,080 --> 00:33:55,020
As the day wore on and the evening came on and began to get dark,

442
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:59,060
well, then you begin to wonder, now, where in the hell are they?

443
00:33:59,620 --> 00:34:02,620
And why aren't they up here, you know, to retrieve us?

444
00:34:03,420 --> 00:34:08,580
Within two hours, the Johnston, the Hole, and the escort ship Roberts had been sunk,

445
00:34:09,040 --> 00:34:11,660
and the survivors were scattered over miles of ocean.

446
00:34:12,380 --> 00:34:16,860
But the U.S. Naval Command was fearful that if a ship should find them and stop,

447
00:34:16,861 --> 00:34:19,520
it would be a sitting target for enemy submarines.

448
00:34:20,080 --> 00:34:24,500
They issued a terrible order, permission denied to save survivors.

449
00:34:25,440 --> 00:34:28,620
Our task force commander knew exactly where we were.

450
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:31,100
He knew we were in the water.

451
00:34:31,740 --> 00:34:36,560
Other ships were asking permission to come back and get us, and it was denied.

452
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:43,000
The men had sacrificed themselves to save the carriers and protect the landings at Leyte Gulf.

453
00:34:43,700 --> 00:34:48,700
For the crews of the destroyers abandoned at sea, the horror was far from over.

454
00:34:48,701 --> 00:34:54,000
We saw our first shark about 3 o'clock that afternoon.

455
00:34:57,660 --> 00:35:00,980
Somebody would scream out when one of them had a hold of them.

456
00:35:01,680 --> 00:35:07,140
And we had a chief petty officer that one of them got him in one of these thighs.

457
00:35:08,060 --> 00:35:11,660
And then just a few minutes afterward, got him in the other thigh.

458
00:35:13,020 --> 00:35:18,320
And so when they'd scream out, you knew that a shark was hitting somebody.

459
00:35:18,700 --> 00:35:23,820
For the next two days, the men fought the sharks and the elements.

460
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:28,480
The water was cool at night, but the sun was hot in daytime.

461
00:35:29,260 --> 00:35:33,620
And, of course, we were from here up, you know, getting burnt real bad.

462
00:35:34,620 --> 00:35:37,600
This little float wasn't very big that we had.

463
00:35:37,720 --> 00:35:43,500
And we put the wounded, most wounded, inside that,

464
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:47,300
which was a couple of guys that was burnt real bad and couldn't see.

465
00:35:47,301 --> 00:35:52,240
And then dark or just a little bit after dark, both of them died.

466
00:35:52,840 --> 00:35:56,980
So we let them slip beneath the surface.

467
00:35:58,860 --> 00:36:03,400
When finally rescued, out of the combined crews of over 600,

468
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:08,980
only 147 survived from the Johnston and 86 from the hull.

469
00:36:10,200 --> 00:36:15,220
The heroism of the destroyer crews was summed up by an official naval historian.

470
00:36:15,221 --> 00:36:24,020
In no engagement of its entire history has the United States Navy shown more gallantry, guts and gumption.

471
00:36:27,180 --> 00:36:31,380
Following this victory, the Philippines were successfully recaptured.

472
00:36:32,040 --> 00:36:34,680
General MacArthur had fulfilled his promise to return.

473
00:36:35,260 --> 00:36:38,040
A destroyer was there to help fulfill that vow.

474
00:36:38,760 --> 00:36:43,260
But for all its armament, the destroyers were small and vulnerable warships.

475
00:36:43,261 --> 00:36:49,000
And it was not only from the hands of their enemies that the men of the destroyers faced death.

476
00:36:49,820 --> 00:36:54,060
Sometimes, nature would unleash itself with devastating consequences.

477
00:36:55,160 --> 00:36:59,700
The Battle of Letty Gulf began on October 23, 1944.

478
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:03,100
Known as the Second Battle of the Philippine Sea,

479
00:37:03,480 --> 00:37:09,120
it was the largest naval battle in history and the last major naval engagement of World War II.

480
00:37:09,840 --> 00:37:11,640
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

481
00:37:13,260 --> 00:37:22,420
By late 1944, the Fletcher-class destroyers were now coming off the slipways at an incredible

482
00:37:22,421 --> 00:37:23,800
rate of four each month.

483
00:37:24,100 --> 00:37:28,660
As soon as they had their shakedown crews, they were hurled into the bloody battle for

484
00:37:28,661 --> 00:37:29,361
the Pacific.

485
00:37:31,320 --> 00:37:36,700
American forces slowly recaptured each island, but the price in lives kept going up.

486
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:41,500
With the Japanese refusing to surrender, it was a case of kill or be killed.

487
00:37:41,501 --> 00:37:48,500
But for the men of the destroyers, death sometimes came from forces which even they could not fight.

488
00:37:52,500 --> 00:37:59,500
The USS Spence was part of the escort for Task Force 38 during the autumn storms of 1944.

489
00:38:00,500 --> 00:38:05,500
If you was watching the ship, it put a clear out of sight where it would go below the waves, you know, and you think,

490
00:38:05,501 --> 00:38:08,520
you think, well, it's gone, then next time it'd be up above you.

491
00:38:08,820 --> 00:38:10,580
50, 60 feet or more.

492
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:16,800
Sometimes wind was so strong, if you'd get into the wind, you couldn't breathe.

493
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:21,380
The next day, the weather became worse.

494
00:38:21,880 --> 00:38:23,860
Then the storm became a typhoon.

495
00:38:24,500 --> 00:38:29,320
In winds of over 100 miles per hour, huge quantities of water swamped the Spence.

496
00:38:29,321 --> 00:38:32,180
I was on the watch on that

497
00:38:32,181 --> 00:38:33,820
and I had all the

498
00:38:33,821 --> 00:38:36,300
all kinds of rain gear on

499
00:38:36,301 --> 00:38:38,520
and everything and the earphones

500
00:38:38,521 --> 00:38:40,580
and you know

501
00:38:40,581 --> 00:38:42,300
it seemed awful funny

502
00:38:42,301 --> 00:38:44,380
like when the ship laid over on its side

503
00:38:44,381 --> 00:38:45,720
and you're walking on the side of it

504
00:38:45,721 --> 00:38:47,800
and then all of a sudden it turns all the way over

505
00:38:47,801 --> 00:38:49,220
and

506
00:38:49,221 --> 00:38:51,200
throwed me out in the water

507
00:38:51,201 --> 00:38:52,880
I didn't

508
00:38:52,881 --> 00:38:55,360
dream of it being a storm

509
00:38:55,361 --> 00:38:57,640
enough to sink any ship

510
00:38:57,641 --> 00:39:00,320
any man a warship anyway.

511
00:39:02,600 --> 00:39:07,040
About 11 a.m., with winds now reaching over 120 knots,

512
00:39:07,440 --> 00:39:08,760
the Spence capsized.

513
00:39:13,320 --> 00:39:18,180
Of the 330 men aboard, only 24 survived.

514
00:39:19,420 --> 00:39:21,800
Everybody that was below deck in the engine room

515
00:39:21,801 --> 00:39:25,000
and the fire rooms and that, they never had a chance.

516
00:39:25,001 --> 00:39:29,480
To chip upside down, there's no way in the world they can ever get out.

517
00:39:30,740 --> 00:39:33,900
Two other destroyers also sank in the typhoon,

518
00:39:34,320 --> 00:39:37,400
and the mighty carrier Hornet had its flight deck ripped apart.

519
00:39:38,940 --> 00:39:43,640
Nearly 790 officers and men lost their lives during the storm.

520
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:50,160
But the men of the destroyers were about to face a far more deadly onslaught.

521
00:39:50,161 --> 00:39:57,440
during the battle of late golf the japanese desperate to defend their positions turned

522
00:39:57,441 --> 00:40:05,040
their fury into a new form of attack the kamikaze again it was the destroyers that were in the front

523
00:40:05,041 --> 00:40:13,120
line of battle their mission was clear target and kill any kamikaze aircraft in the vicinity of an

524
00:40:13,121 --> 00:40:14,580
aircraft carrier

525
00:40:14,581 --> 00:40:16,660
it's total suicide effort

526
00:40:16,661 --> 00:40:19,740
uh... personal so i couldn't believe it

527
00:40:19,741 --> 00:40:21,440
became

528
00:40:21,441 --> 00:40:22,440
really

529
00:40:22,441 --> 00:40:23,660
information

530
00:40:23,661 --> 00:40:24,580
destroyers

531
00:40:24,581 --> 00:40:30,440
in a circle

532
00:40:30,441 --> 00:40:33,600
flyover

533
00:40:33,601 --> 00:40:36,540
and i would see these planes coming in and

534
00:40:36,541 --> 00:40:37,380
see the

535
00:40:37,381 --> 00:40:40,880
the shells going out

536
00:40:40,881 --> 00:40:47,600
We fired the five-inch guns, the proximity fuse, which meant they would explode and shot them all over the place.

537
00:40:47,601 --> 00:40:52,220
And the 40 millimeters are going out, the 20 millimeters are going out when you get close enough.

538
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:57,000
And you just sit there and say, well, how can you get through it?

539
00:40:57,140 --> 00:41:03,320
There's so much metal up there that they bound to run into something, but it didn't hit the right place.

540
00:41:04,200 --> 00:41:06,340
Then the thing still flew.

541
00:41:06,341 --> 00:41:12,100
So we just grit your teeth and you keep shooting bang bang bang.

542
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:16,540
Finally you'd hit it or it would fly over you and go look for something else.

543
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:26,420
On April 6th, 1945, the USS Bush was on picket duty off the coast of Okinawa

544
00:41:26,421 --> 00:41:30,520
when CIC's radar picked up a large formation of enemy aircraft.

545
00:41:30,521 --> 00:41:41,120
we first spotted them about 50 miles away we saw a pretty large group probably 10 to 20 planes

546
00:41:41,121 --> 00:41:49,100
and here's another group behind that and then almost immediately a third group and then a

547
00:41:49,101 --> 00:41:56,240
fourth group and so we knew this was going to be a big one within minutes the bush knew that they

548
00:41:56,241 --> 00:41:57,883
were the target.

549
00:41:57,884 --> 00:42:05,480
We had a plane coming in at low on the water, on our bow, right before us.

550
00:42:06,420 --> 00:42:14,180
We fired everything, the 5-inch, the 40s, the 20s, all were firing, and didn't stop him,

551
00:42:14,181 --> 00:42:21,380
a very skilled pilot, and he knew what he was doing, and he hit us exactly right in the spot,

552
00:42:21,381 --> 00:42:28,800
the most perfect spot possible, and that is right between the two stacks, right at the water line.

553
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:39,580
Then about a half hour later, another one came in a little higher and peeled off and came in

554
00:42:39,581 --> 00:42:43,860
